  },
  {
    factor: 'Free-time discipline',
    effect: 'Demurrage and detention apply after free time',
    note: 'Pre-clear through Al-Nadeeb to avoid per-day charges',
    confidence: 'LOW',
  },
  {
    factor: 'Documents and Al-Nadeeb readiness',
    effect: 'Late or wrong documents delay clearance and start demurrage',
    note: 'Prepare invoice, packing list, B/L and HS codes before arrival',
    confidence: 'MEDIUM',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does China-to-Qatar LCL cost per CBM?',
    a: 'The published band is $75–$150 per CBM (Sino’s $75 stable quote plus a regional guide’s $80–150 ceiling), with a $70 floor across older snapshots — roughly 3.5× the corridor-wide $20 floor and about 2.3× the UAE’s $30 base bracket. These are indicative collected figures, not carrier quotes; confirm the CFS cutoff, minimum CBM and LCL-specific destination fees at booking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does shipping cost from China to Qatar?',
    a: 'The September 2026 rows: 20ft FCL $4,365–$5,354 and 40ft $6,485–$8,204 from Shanghai (MEDIUM, flagged +~25% month-on-month), a July South-China sheet at $1,750–$2,956 (LOW, pre-escalation), LCL at $75–$150/CBM and air at $3.76–$4.62/kg. Add the ×1.05 statutory stack — 5% duty, no VAT — on the CIF value. Verify every figure with a forwarder before booking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is Qatar’s LCL more expensive than Dubai’s?',
    a: 'Three structural reasons stack up: a single gateway (Hamad Port) with no competing terminal to compress rates, thinner consolidation volume than Jebel Ali so fewer boxes to pool per sailing, and per-CBM handling economics that show up at low volumes when fixed terminal costs are spread across less cargo. The result is a $70+ floor against the UAE’s $30 base bracket. The gap is a market-structure story, not a different ocean.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does shipping from China to Qatar take?',
    a: 'The route-data baseline is a typical 22 days with a 15–30 day window. A secondary market guide gives a 22–28 day sea bracket and 2–4 days by air. One LCL sheet quotes 9–17 days for its service — service-specific and unverified; treat the 22-day typical as the planning number and ask which service a given quote uses. Air at 3–7 days is the wide-end alternative.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is the Qatar landed cost calculated?',
    a: 'On a $10,000 CIF: 5% duty is $500 and there is no general VAT on imports in the current framework, so the statutory landed cost is $10,500 — CIF × 1.05. Destination THC, documentation, Al-Nadeeb clearance, inland trucking and any demurrage or detention are added on top and are not quantified in public snapshots; request them itemised.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Qatar charge VAT on top of import duty?',
    a: 'No — Qatar’s stack stops at ×1.05 (5% duty, 0% general VAT), so a $10,000 CIF clears at $10,500 versus $12,075 in Saudi Arabia (×1.2075) and $11,025 in the UAE (×1.1025). That is a $1,575 saving versus Saudi Arabia and $525 versus the UAE on the same base. Verify the current VAT position with the General Authority of Customs before finalising a quote — frameworks can change.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is air freight from China to Doha ever cheaper than LCL?',
    a: 'Rarely per unit, but the trap cuts both ways. Air bills on chargeable weight: 2 CBM becomes 333.33 kg, so at $3.76–$4.62/kg the air bill runs about $1,253–$1,540 versus LCL at roughly $140–$300 — a 4–11× gap. Air can still win when LCL minimums bite (at 0.5 CBM the multiple narrows to about 2–6×), when stock-out or delay costs exceed roughly $50–$90 per day, or when value density is high. Run the per-shipment arithmetic, not the per-kg headline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is FCL or LCL better for shipping to Hamad Port?',
    a: 'Quote both — the published crossover spreads from about 12–26 CBM (July South-China sheet) to about 29–71 CBM (September Shanghai sheet), because the two sheets disagree on FCL levels. Below ~12 CBM LCL is competitive on any reading; above ~25 CBM push hard for FCL terms; in between, the quote does the deciding. Destination fees, which fall more heavily on LCL, push the practical crossover down.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to Qatar cargo if the Strait of Hormuz is disrupted?',
    a: 'Qatar formalised alternatives during the 2026 disruption: its Ministry of Transport designated Omani and UAE ports for Hamad-bound cargo — Sohar and Duqm as the first alternative, Fujairah and Khorfakkan as the second — with overland legs into Qatar, and Windward’s March 2026 update describes Sohar, Salalah and Duqm as Qatar’s designated alternatives. Budget a transit premium and ask any forwarder how they route around the strait before a crisis does it for them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which port and airport should the landed-cost quote use?',
    a: 'Sea freight: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Qatar’s sole commercial container gateway since 2017. Air: Doha airport (DOH). The legacy Doha Port no longer takes commercial container traffic, so a quote that still routes boxes through “Doha Port” is out of date — ask the forwarder to name the terminal. On the bill of lading, QAHMD is the code that matters.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What hidden fees should I expect on a Qatar shipment?',
    a: 'Duty is the only quantified statutory line at 5% of CIF; every other fee amount is unpublished, so budget the destination stack as an unknown add-on: origin charges, destination THC and port charges, documentation and bill of lading fees, customs brokerage, Al-Nadeeb processing, inspection if selected, inland transport, cargo insurance if elected, and demurrage or detention after free time. Ask for an itemised schedule rather than a single all-in number.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I reduce the landed cost on the China-to-Qatar route?',
    a: 'The moves that matter most: consolidate past the practical crossover so LCL’s per-CBM pricing flips into a flat box rate; compare FCL and LCL all-in quotes at the same destination week; classify the HS code correctly before booking; screen restricted goods early; file the Al-Nadeeb declaration before arrival to protect free time; and negotiate the destination fee schedule. The 0% VAT saving is already in your favour — the controllable lines are freight basis and destination fees.',
  },
];

const organization = {
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
  name: 'WorldFreightHub',
  url: SITE_URL,
  logo: {
    '@type': 'ImageObject',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/favicon.svg`,
  },
};

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'Article',
    data: {
      headline: 'Shipping Cost & Transit Time from China to Qatar: LCL Floor, 0% VAT and Hamad Port (September 2026)',
      description:
        'China-to-Qatar shipping cost and transit time: the $70/CBM LCL floor, the ×1.05 zero-VAT landing stack, chargeable-weight maths for air freight, and Hamad Port’s single-gateway risk map.',
      datePublished: '2026-09-01',
      dateModified: '2026-09-16',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        '@type': 'WebPage',
        '@id': pageUrl,
      },
      author: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'WorldFreightHub',
        url: SITE_URL,
      },
      publisher: {
        '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
      },
      image: `${SITE_URL}/og.png`,
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: faq.q,
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: faq.a,
        },
      })),
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'Organization',
    data: organization,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Shipping Cost & Transit Time from China to Qatar: LCL Floor, 0% VAT and Hamad Port (September 2026)"
  description="China-to-Qatar shipping cost and transit time: the $70/CBM LCL floor, the ×1.05 zero-VAT landing stack, chargeable-weight maths for air freight, and Hamad Port’s single-gateway risk map."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Shipping cost & transit time China to Qatar' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping cost &amp; transit time from China to Qatar: the LCL floor, the zero-VAT landing, and Hamad Port</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Qatar is the easiest GCC landing on paper — 5% duty and no general VAT, so a $10,000 CIF
        clears at $10,500 — and one of the harder ones to move to, because every container enters
        through a single port on a thin, war-premium lane. This page does the maths both ways: the
        $70/CBM LCL floor that sits 3.5× above the corridor, the chargeable-weight trap that flips
        small shipments toward air, and the single-gateway risk map that matters when the Strait
        of Hormuz does not cooperate.
      </p>

      <section class="section container">
        <blockquote class="tldr">
          <p>
            <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Qatar’s ×1.05 stack (5% duty, 0% VAT) lands $10,000 CIF at
            $10,500 — $1,575 under Saudi Arabia and $525 under the UAE — but the tax saving is not
            free money on small volumes: the published LCL floor is $70/CBM, 3.5× the corridor’s
            $20 floor, and at 2 CBM the air alternative ($1,253&ndash;$1,540 chargeable) runs
            4&ndash;11× the LCL bill ($140&ndash;$300). Hamad Port is the only container gateway,
            with Sohar, Salalah and Duqm formalised as crisis alternatives.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified rules from indicative market snapshots. The 5% duty and
        0% VAT lines are verified; rate rows range from MEDIUM (dated 2026 quote sets) to LOW
        (pre-escalation or bundled-basis figures). Treat every LOW figure as indicative and verify
        with your carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">General VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">None <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Statutory stack</span>
          <span class="fact-value">CIF × 1.05 <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Typical 22 days <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Container gateway</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Hamad Port (QAHMD) <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">LCL floor</span>
          <span class="fact-value">$70/CBM <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. The three layers of a China-to-Qatar quote — and the one most sellers compare</h2>
    <p>
      Every China-to-Qatar quote contains three layers. <strong>The international move</strong>:
      ocean freight for FCL or LCL, or air freight billed on chargeable weight. <strong>The
      statutory stack</strong>: 5% duty on CIF, with no general VAT on imports in the current
      framework — the ×1.05 position that makes Qatar one of the two lightest GCC landings
      alongside Kuwait. <strong>The destination fee stack</strong>: terminal handling,
      documentation, brokerage, Al-Nadeeb processing, inland transport, and demurrage or detention
      if free time runs out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sellers compare the first layer, because it is the number on the quote. On Qatar that habit
      is riskier than usual, for two reasons. The lane is thin — fewer sailings, common
      transshipment, a single container gateway — so the freight layer carries a small-market
      premium that a Dubai-quoted benchmark will not show. And the tax layer, the one place Qatar
      beats its neighbours by design, only pays off if your freight basis does not quietly eat the
      difference. This page prices all three layers separately so the trade is visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. The China→Qatar rate card: LCL floor, FCL spread, and the air band</h2>
      <p>
        WHFI v1 — the WorldFreightHub Freight Index — now carries seven China→Qatar rows covering
        FCL, LCL and air. Read the confidence column as carefully as the dollars: the September
        Shanghai quotes are MEDIUM (dated, but forwarder-sourced), while the South-China FCL sheet
        and the platform figures are LOW and on different bases. The spread between them is part
        of the story, not noise to average away.
      </p>

      <h3>FCL by origin and size</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'ref', label: 'Ref' },
          { key: 'lane', label: 'Lane' },
          { key: 'size', label: 'Size' },
          { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Indicative range' },
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={fclRateRows}
        caption="Port-to-port planning ranges where stated; platform figures are bundled. Ref IDs map to the WHFI v1 rate register (r-301 to r-307). Verify at booking."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — FCL rate rows" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">LCL and air</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'ref', label: 'Ref' },
          { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode / lane' },
          { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Indicative range' },
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={lclAirRows}
        caption="LCL and air ranges move with volume bracket, weight tier and season. Verify with a forwarder before quoting onward."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — LCL & air rate rows" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Why the spread is so wide:</strong> the September Shanghai quotes carry a Hormuz
        risk premium the July South-China sheet predates, and the platform rows bundle services
        the port-to-port rows exclude. A 2.5× spread across sources is what a strained corridor
        looks like — treat the low end as the market that was, and the high end as the market the
        source can defend today.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Why Qatar’s LCL floor sits at 3.5× the corridor’s</h2>
    <p>
      The corridor-wide LCL benchmark runs $20–$180/CBM with a $20 floor; Qatar’s published floor
      is $70 — a 3.5× gap on the same nominal product. That gap is not a rounding error, and it is
      not explained by distance. Doha is not meaningfully further from Shanghai than Dubai is.
      What differs is market structure, and it shows up in three places.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Single gateway, zero competition.</strong> All commercial container cargo clears
        through Hamad Port. Jebel Ali’s rate compression comes from multiple terminal operations,
        frequent sailings and a deep pool of consolidators; Hamad has one operating model and a
        thinner carrier roster, so there is less tension pulling per-unit pricing down.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thin consolidation volume.</strong> LCL is a pooling business. Fewer onward boxes
        per sailing means a small shipment waits longer to fill a consolidation, carries higher
        minimum charges, or both. The published LCL window (quoted at 9–17 days by one source, but
        the lane’s planning number runs longer) is only as good as the next consolidation date,
        which is why this page keeps LCL at LOW confidence inside a MEDIUM lane.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Terminal economics at low volume.</strong> Fixed terminal and CFS handling costs
        are spread across fewer CBM in Qatar than in Dubai. The specific fee amounts at Hamad are
        not published in our snapshot — so we do not quote a number — but the direction is
        structural, and it is the same force keeping the floor at $70+ rather than $30.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The practical consequence: on small shipments, the corridor’s cheapest LCL market and its
      most expensive are often compared as if they were interchangeable. They are not. If your
      goods are LCL-sized and Qatar-bound, budget from the $70–$150 band, and ask the two
      questions that actually move the number: what is the CFS cutoff and next consolidation
      sailing, and what is the minimum CBM for the service.
    </p>
    <SourceList sources={floorSources} title="Sources — LCL floor and market structure" />
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. The chargeable-weight trap: when air to Doha beats LCL</h2>
      <p>
        Air freight does not bill the weight on your scale. It bills the higher of actual and
        volumetric weight, and the air convention — roughly 1 CBM per 167 kg (the ÷6000 divisor)
        — turns bulky light cargo into heavy invoices. Two cubic metres of foam-packed consumer
        goods weighing 120 kg physically become <strong>333.33 kg chargeable</strong> — a 2.8×
        uplift that decides the mode question before you do.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'volume', label: 'Volume' },
          { key: 'chargeable', label: 'Chargeable weight (÷6000)' },
          { key: 'air', label: 'Air cost at $3.76–$4.62/kg' },
          { key: 'lcl', label: 'LCL cost at $70–$150/CBM' },
          { key: 'reading', label: 'Read' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={chargeableWeightRows}
        caption="Illustrative arithmetic from the published ranges. Actual quotes vary with airports, service type and season."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — chargeable-weight maths" />

      <p class="mt-2">
        Look at the shape of the table, not just the totals. At 2 CBM the air bill is roughly
        $1,253–$1,540 against LCL’s $140–$300 — a gap of about $950–$1,400. That premium buys back
        two to three weeks of transit. It pays when a stock-out, a project deadline or
        financing-inventory cost exceeds roughly $50–$90 per day of delay — and it never pays for
        slow-moving bulk. At 0.5 CBM the story flips subtly: LCL minimums stop the per-CBM maths
        from helping, and the air multiple narrows to about 2–6×, which is why small urgent
        parcels should compare consolidated air (and courier) against LCL directly rather than
        assuming sea is always cheaper.
      </p>
      <p>
        One more layer: DDP air and express products (which bundle clearance, duty and delivery)
        ran above the airport-to-airport band in the earlier snapshot — around $4.70–$8.50/kg —
        and that is the honest comparison for door-to-door air, not the raw per-kg rate. If a
        seller quotes you “$3.90/kg” without saying whether it is airport-to-airport or delivered,
        you do not yet have a price. The <a href="/middleeast/air-freight-from-china-to-qatar/">Qatar
        air freight guide</a> covers the mode mechanics in full.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Build CIF, then apply Qatar’s ×1.05: the $10,500 landing</h2>
    <p>
      Qatar’s duty base is CIF — cost, insurance and freight — so the first task is assembling a
      defensible CIF value, and the second is the short statutory arithmetic: 5% duty, no general
      VAT, <code>CIF × 1.05</code>. Freight and insurance are inside the base, so a higher freight
      quote raises duty as well, even though there is no VAT layer to compound it.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 1 — Assemble a defensible CIF value</h3>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'layer', label: 'Cost layer' },
        { key: 'calc', label: 'How to obtain it' },
        { key: 'amount', label: 'Indicative amount' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={[
        { layer: 'Goods / invoice value', calc: 'From the supplier invoice', amount: 'Use the actual declared value', note: 'The starting point for Qatar customs duty', confidence: 'LOW' },
        { layer: 'Freight (sea or air)', calc: 'Carrier / forwarder quote', amount: 'See the rate card above', note: 'Inside the duty base — freight is not tax-free', confidence: 'LOW' },
        { layer: 'Insurance', calc: 'Insurer or forwarder quote', amount: 'Not published in the snapshot', note: 'Usually a small percentage of cargo value', confidence: 'LOW' },
        { layer: 'CIF value', calc: 'Cost + insurance + freight', amount: '$10,000.00 illustrative', note: 'Illustrative round figure; replace it with your CIF', confidence: 'LOW' },
        { layer: 'Customs duty (baseline)', calc: 'CIF × 5%', amount: '$500.00', note: 'GCC Common External Tariff, ad valorem on CIF', confidence: 'MEDIUM' },
        { layer: 'General VAT', calc: 'No general VAT on imports', amount: '$0.00', note: 'Qatar has no general import VAT in the current framework', confidence: 'MEDIUM' },
        { layer: 'Statutory landed cost', calc: 'CIF × 1.05', amount: '$10,500.00', note: '= 1.05 × CIF at the baseline duty rate', confidence: 'MEDIUM' },
        { layer: 'Destination fees', calc: 'THC + docs + clearance/Al-Nadeeb + inland + demurrage/detention', amount: 'Not published — request schedule', note: 'Added on top of the statutory stack', confidence: 'LOW' },
        { layer: 'Total landed cost', calc: 'Statutory landed cost + destination fees', amount: '$10,500.00 + destination fees', note: 'The all-in figure to benchmark quotes against', confidence: 'MEDIUM' },
      ]}
      caption="CIF 10,000 USD is an illustrative round figure only. Replace it with your actual declared value, freight and insurance."
    />
    <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — CIF and statutory layers" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>One-line formula:</strong> <code>CIF × 1.05</code> — duty only, no VAT layer. The
      discipline note still applies: under-declaring CIF invites revaluation and penalties, and a
      correction flows straight through the duty line.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. What 0% VAT is worth: the $10,000 CIF comparison against Saudi Arabia</h2>
      <p>
        The zero-VAT position is Qatar’s headline advantage, and it is worth putting a number on
        it across the corridor — same CIF base, four destinations:
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'destination', label: 'Destination' },
          { key: 'stack', label: 'Statutory stack' },
          { key: 'landed', label: 'Landed on $10,000 CIF' },
          { key: 'gap', label: 'Gap vs Qatar' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={stackCompareRows}
        caption="Same $10,000 CIF across four GCC destinations. Only Qatar and Kuwait currently stop at ×1.05."
      />
      <SourceList sources={[...taxSources, ...sourcesByIds(['kuwait-customs'])]} title="Sources — GCC stack comparison" />

      <p class="mt-2">
        So the saving is <strong>$1,575 against Saudi Arabia</strong> and $525 against the UAE on
        the same $10,000 base. Now the part most VAT-first arguments miss: on small shipments, the
        freight layer can claw back a slice of that. The LCL floor premium is $50/CBM against the
        corridor floor ($70 − $20), so at 15 CBM the premium alone is $750 — roughly half the
        Saudi saving. Below about 5 CBM the premium is under $250 and barely dents it. Qatar still
        wins the comparison for most pairs of numbers, but the margin is thinner than the
        raw VAT gap suggests, and the thinner your volume, the smaller the freight noise.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two caveats to carry forward. Duty lines vary by HS code — certain essential foods can sit
        at 0%, and tobacco and alcohol carry much higher rates where permitted — so classify
        before quoting. And VAT frameworks change: Qatar currently has no general import VAT, but
        several GCC neighbours have introduced theirs on a similar template. Verify the current
        position with the General Authority of Customs before locking a landed-cost model to 0%.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. FCL vs LCL to Hamad Port: where the crossover actually sits</h2>
    <p>
      The GCC floor for this argument is “cross over at about 15 CBM”. On Qatar’s published
      numbers, that heuristic is not wrong so much as under-specified — because the two rate
      sheets for this lane disagree enough to put the crossover anywhere between roughly 12 and
      71 CBM.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
        { key: 'fcl', label: 'FCL' },
        { key: 'lcl', label: 'LCL' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={fclVsLclRows}
      caption="FCL/LCL comparison on the WHFI v1 rows. The crossover output is a range, not a point."
    />
    <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — FCL/LCL inputs" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">The arithmetic, both ways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>September Shanghai sheet:</strong> $4,365–$5,354 ÷ $75–$150 = roughly
        <strong>29–71 CBM</strong>. On this sheet LCL’s per-CBM maths stays competitive past a full
        20ft cube (~33 CBM) — a result that should make you suspicious of any single-sheet
        crossover claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>July South-China sheet:</strong> $1,750–$1,954 ÷ $75–$150 = roughly
        <strong>12–26 CBM</strong>, which lands right on the conventional heuristic.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both arithmetic lines are correct; the inputs differ. That is the whole finding: on Qatar,
      the crossover is not a property of the lane, it is a property of the rate sheet you can
      actually get. Two forces then bend the practical decision. Destination fees fall harder on
      LCL (CFS deconsolidation, extra handling), pushing the real-world crossover down — favor the
      box earlier than the headline maths says. The September war-premium FCL quotes push it up —
      LCL looks cheaper for longer than it should. The only defensible instruction is to quote
      both at the same destination in the same week, and to treat 15 CBM as a hypothesis, not a
      law. Past roughly 25 CBM, fight for FCL terms; below roughly 12 CBM, LCL is competitive on
      any reading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>8. Hamad Port, Doha airport, and the single-gateway risk map</h2>
      <p>
        Qatar’s port question has a one-line answer — <strong>Hamad Port ({unlocode})</strong> — and a
        longer implication. Opened in 2017 to replace the old Doha port, Hamad is the country’s
        sole commercial container gateway, operated by Mwani Qatar, with a design capacity around
        7.5 million TEU. The single-gateway structure means no in-country fallback terminal, wider
        schedule windows than the big hubs, and per-shipment costs that do not get absorbed by
        competing facilities.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 2026 Strait of Hormuz disruption turned that theoretical concentration risk into an
        operating reality. Qatar’s Ministry of Transport formalised alternative routings for
        Hamad-bound cargo — naming Sohar and Duqm as the first alternative and Fujairah and
        Khorfakkan as the second, with overland legs into Qatar — while Windward’s March 2026
        maritime update describes Sohar, Salalah and Duqm as Qatar’s designated alternatives.
        For an importer, three practical consequences follow:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Ask how the routing works before booking.</strong> A Hamad quote built on a
          direct strait transit and one built on an Omani discharge plus truck bridge are
          different products with different days and dollars. The quote should say which it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Budget a contingency line.</strong> Crisis-era routings add a handling point and
          an overland leg; the September Shanghai FCL quote already flags a Hormuz risk premium on
          the lane. Keep a buffer, not a hope.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Retire the Doha Port legacy.</strong> The old port no longer takes commercial
          container traffic. A forwarder quoting boxes through “Doha Port” is working from
          outdated routing — a cheap tell for who has refreshed their lane data.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Air cargo has its own single gateway: Doha international (DOH), the natural fit for the
        urgent, light and high-value volume that would otherwise wait for the next consolidation
        or the next sailing. The port-and-hinterland layer is covered in more depth in the
        <a href="/middleeast/hamad-port/">Hamad Port guide</a> and the
        <a href="/middleeast/shipping-from-china-to-qatar/">China→Qatar route pillar</a>.
      </p>
      <SourceList sources={[...gatewaySources, ...transitSources]} title="Sources — Hamad Port & crisis routing" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>9. Qatar’s hidden fee stack: demurrage, storage, and what is not published</h2>
    <p>
      Duty is the only quantified statutory line for Qatar; everything else is a schedule you have
      to request. The recurring fee families and the operational levers that move them:
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
        { key: 'effect', label: 'Effect on cost or time' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={factorRows}
      caption="Operational context. Only 5% duty and no general VAT are verified — unquantified fees are LOW confidence and require a fee schedule."
    />
    <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — cost & risk factors" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage and detention on a single-gateway lane</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the terminal’s charge when a container overstays free time
      inside the port; <strong>detention</strong> is the carrier’s charge when the box overstays
      free time outside it. On a single-gateway market these clocks matter more, not less: when
      everything must clear through Hamad, a peak-season queue or a slow inspection can start the
      meter on multiple containers at once. The schedules are unpublished in our snapshot — ask
      for both before booking, and pre-clear the Al-Nadeeb declaration so the box is released
      rather than parked.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-3">Cost discipline for a Qatar shipment</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Quote FCL and LCL on the same week and destination before choosing — the crossover moves with the sheet.</li>
      <li>Treat the LCL floor as $70+ and budget the CFS/minimum-charge detail before committing small volumes.</li>
      <li>Classify the HS code and screen restricted goods before booking.</li>
      <li>File the Al-Nadeeb declaration before the vessel arrives to protect free time.</li>
      <li>Ask which routing your Hamad quote assumes, and price the contingency.</li>
      <li>Negotiate the destination fee schedule, not just the headline rate.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>10. Compliance on the Qatar lane: duty lines, Al-Nadeeb, and restricted goods</h2>
      <p>
        Qatar’s baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF value</strong> under the GCC Common
        External Tariff, and the framework currently carries <strong>no general VAT on
        imports</strong> — the ×1.05 stack. Specific goods still move off the baseline: certain
        essential foods sit at 0%, and tobacco and alcohol carry rates far above 5% where
        permitted at all. The HS code is the first cost decision and it is made before quoting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The clearance flow runs through <strong>Al-Nadeeb</strong>, the General Authority of
        Customs’ electronic system:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Classify the goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting or booking.</li>
        <li>Confirm importer registration, permits and any restricted-commodity approval before shipment.</li>
        <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or air waybill, and certificate of origin.</li>
        <li>File the <strong>Al-Nadeeb</strong> declaration through the General Authority of Customs.</li>
        <li>Pay the assessed 5% duty on CIF, pass inspection if selected, and take delivery.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Two frequent import confusions, cleared. <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States
        requirement and does not apply to Qatar — the advance-filing step here is Al-Nadeeb.
        And <strong>SABER/SASO certification is a Saudi regime</strong>; Qatar runs its own
        standards and restricted-goods screening, which is strict on a defined list of products
        and can hold a release until documentation is resolved. The commodity screen is a schedule
        item you check before booking, not after.
      </p>
      <SourceList sources={[...taxSources, ...qatarPortSources]} title="Sources — Qatar customs & compliance" />
    </div>
  </section>

