  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The LCL floor sits at $70/CBM — 3.5× the $20 corridor floor and about 2.3× the UAE’s $30 base bracket — a market-structure premium, not a distance one.</li>
      <li>Zero VAT is worth $1,575 against Saudi Arabia and $525 against the UAE on $10,000 CIF — but a $50/CBM floor premium on 15 CBM of LCL ($750) eats roughly half the Saudi saving.</li>
      <li>Chargeable weight is the air trap: 2 CBM bills 333.33 kg, so air runs $1,253–$1,540 against LCL’s $140–$300 (4–11×); at 0.5 CBM the multiple narrows to about 2–6× as LCL minimums bite.</li>
      <li>The FCL/LCL crossover spreads from roughly 12–26 CBM to 29–71 CBM across published sheets — quote both and treat 15 CBM as a hypothesis.</li>
      <li>Hamad Port (QAHMD) is Qatar’s sole container gateway; when Hormuz strains, Sohar, Salalah and Duqm are the formalised alternatives, so ask every quote which routing it assumes.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, Qatar’s 0% VAT is a real advantage that deserves to be taken seriously for anything above LCL scale, but it is oversold for small shippers: the $70/CBM LCL floor can claw back half the $1,575 Saudi saving at 15 CBM, so I would make the forwarder quote the tax-inclusive landing and the per-CBM freight before believing the VAT headline.</p>
    <ul><li>×1.05 lands $10,000 CIF at $10,500 — $1,575 under Saudi Arabia and $525 under the UAE, on identical goods.</li><li>The floor premium is $50/CBM ($70 − $20); at 15 CBM it is $750, roughly half the Saudi saving, and at sub-5-CBM volumes it is under $250 — which is where the tax case is strongest.</li><li>The crossover spread of 12–71 CBM across sheets means any single “FCL wins at 15 CBM” claim on this lane is a guess until two quotes are on the table.</li></ul>
    <p class="note"><em>Disclosure: This is the author's editorial judgment based on the published data on this page — not an independently verified fact.</em></p>
  </section>

